   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hello Jerome,<br><br>Thank you a lot for your answer.<br>So I understand that amplitude unities in oscillo() and spec() functions, when not normalized, depend exclusively of the recorder with the one the sound file was recorded, that is, the amplitude units with the ones it is recording (Pa, W/m2, mV, etc.). Am I right?<br> <br>Thanks a lot,<br>Irene\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"> </p></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On Tue, Nov 4, 2014 at 3:55 AM, Jerome Sueur <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">su...@mnhn.fr</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\">\r\n  \r\n    \r\n  \r\n  <div bgcolor=\"#FFFFFF\" text=\"#000000\">\r\n    <div>Le 03/11/2014 22:38, Irene T. Roca a\r\n      écrit :<br>\r\n    </div>\r\n    <blockquote type=\"cite\"><span>\r\n      <div dir=\"ltr\">\r\n        <p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0cm;margin-bottom:.0001pt;line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;background-image:initial;background-repeat:initial\">Hello,</span><span><u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n        <p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0.0001pt;line-height:normal;background-image:initial;background-repeat:initial\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:Arial,sans-serif\"> </span></p>\r\n        <p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0.0001pt;line-height:normal;background-image:initial;background-repeat:initial\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:Arial,sans-serif\">I&#39;m\r\n            working\r\n            with the function “oscillo” in {seewave} and I’ll like to\r\n            know which are the\r\n            amplitude units that it’s using. When I write the\r\n            code: oscillo(a,\r\n            yaxt=&quot;l&quot;, xaxt=&quot;s&quot;) I get amplitude values in some cases\r\n            varying from -30000 to 30000. <u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n        <p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0.0001pt;line-height:normal;background-image:initial;background-repeat:initial\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:Arial,sans-serif\"> </span></p>\r\n        <p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0.0001pt;line-height:normal;background-image:initial;background-repeat:initial\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:Arial,sans-serif\">Thank\r\n            you\r\n            very much<u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n        <p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0.0001pt;line-height:normal;background-image:initial;background-repeat:initial\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:Arial,sans-serif\">Irene<u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n        <p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"FR-CA\"> </span></p>\r\n      </div>\r\n      -- <br></span>\r\n      You received this message because you are subscribed to the Google\r\n      Groups &quot;seewave&quot; group.<br>\r\n      To unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it,\r\n      send an email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">seewave+u...@googlegroups.com</a>.<span><br>\r\n      For more options, visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/optout\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/optout&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792472382703000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3HLbAtq347H07LJ-_hcwCO\">https://groups.google.com/d/optout</a>.<br>\r\n    </span></blockquote>\r\n    Hello,<br>\r\n    <br>\r\n    The oscillo() function does not have an y-axis unit as, as you\r\n    suggested, the input can vary on any range depending on the source\r\n    file format. The values are often not calibrated, ie no absolute\r\n    pressure (Pa) or electrical (mV) can be obtained. In most cases, it\r\n    is therefore better to think this axis as a relative amplitude scale\r\n    without unit.<br>\r\n    <br>\r\n    HTH<span><font color=\"#888888\"><br>\r\n    <br>\r\n    Jerome<br>\r\n  </font></span></div></blockquote></div></div>"
